WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.560
What do a back tattoo, Pulp Fiction, and Donald
Glover have in common?

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.664
They're all Easter eggs you might've missed
in the MCU.

00:00:07.664 --> 00:00:13.327
It was the unexpected success of Iron Man
that gave Marvel Studios the jump-start it needed.

00:00:13.327 --> 00:00:17.499
That said, it also helped that the filmmakers
sneaked in little references here and there

00:00:17.499 --> 00:00:18.970
to a larger universe.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:23.859
This includes a prop that only eagle-eyed
viewers caught – the shield of World War

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:25.599
II hero Captain America.

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:29.659
It's partially constructed and sitting idly
on Tony Stark's work desk.

00:00:29.659 --> 00:00:35.239
In a 2010 interview with MTV, director Jon
Favreau explained that the movie's inclusion

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:39.810
of the iconic symbol was nothing more than
a visual gag, at least at first.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:40.810
He explained,

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:45.200
"An [Industrial Light and Magic] artist put
it in there as a joke to us for our cineSync

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.640
sessions, when we're approving visual effects."

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.928
They liked it enough that they kept it in
the film's final cut.

00:00:50.928 --> 00:00:56.449
In "The Incredible Hulk," Emil Blonsky receives
a special serum that transforms him into the

00:00:56.449 --> 00:01:00.179
Abomination, a monster intended to be the
Hulk's physical match.

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:04.430
The container holding the serum bears a label
attributing its development to a certain Dr.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.530
Reinstein, a name familiar to longtime "Captain
America" readers.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:14.130
"Dr. Reinstein" was actually a fake name used
by Dr. Abraham Erskine, the man who developed

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:19.840
the Super Soldier formula that turned the
scrawny Steve Rogers into America's mightiest champion.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.440
Interestingly, the final cut of The Incredible
Hulk could have featured more than a passing

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.880
reference to the Captain, had things turned
out the way the director originally intended.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:32.920
A deleted scene actually included a cameo
of the Star-Spangled Avenger's frozen body,

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.464
preserved underneath the Arctic ice.

00:01:35.464 --> 00:01:40.770
Aside from introducing new heroes Black Widow
and War Machine, Iron Man 2 also delved deeper

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:45.060
into the Avengers Initiative, exploring how
it would be executed while emphasizing that

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.570
Stark may not be a good addition to the project.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:50.530
"'Prone to self-destructive tendencies' — I
was dying!

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.920
And, please, aren't we all?"

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:57.450
Interestingly, the scene where Fury delivers
the not-so-good news to Stark contains an

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:02.070
early reference to the existence of a hero
who would eventually fight alongside the Avengers:

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:07.350
The sequence features the two men sitting
down in a covert location and discussing Black

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.979
Widow's assessment of Stark.

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:11.039
Beside Stark and Fury are some S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.720
holographic screens that depict a map of the
world, with certain locations marked.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.340
The marker that certainly got a lot of attention,
though, is one that pointed to a seemingly

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:24.645
secret location in Africa: The technologically
advanced, Vibranium-rich nation of Wakanda.

00:02:24.645 --> 00:02:30.090
In the scene where Thor is proclaimed by his
father Odin as Asgard's next king, we see

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.010
their sworn enemies, the Frost Giants, making
their way through the palace walls and into

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.480
the vault where precious artifacts are kept
by the All-Father.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:41.879
Seeking to obtain the Casket of Ancient Winters,
they struck a deal with Thor's trickster brother

00:02:41.879 --> 00:02:44.760
Loki that allowed them to sneak into the palace
undetected.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.190
However, when the invaders attempt to take
the relic, the Destroyer comes to life and

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.340
takes them down.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.890
In the chaos that follows, the Infinity Gauntlet
is shown for a brief moment on screen.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:03:00.420
However, as Hela casually revealed in Thor:
Ragnarok, the Gauntlet in Odin's vault was a counterfeit.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.650
This throwaway line of dialogue was meant
to address a massive plot hole that was created

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.719
when Avengers: Age of Ultron showed the Gauntlet
in the possession of Thanos all along.

00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:14.440
Set during the Second World War, the first
Captain America movie told the tale of how

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:21.019
a scrawny, bullied kid from Brooklyn was gifted
with a superhuman physique to match his superheroic heart.

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:24.550
Initially deemed too expensive and risky to
send out into actual combat, Captain America

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:27.299
soon took the lead in fighting Hitler's men.

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:33.160
Wait a minute — an American adventurer in
an iconic costume going up against German forces?

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.430
If that sounds familiar to you, perhaps you've
seen Raiders of the Lost Ark.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.180
In case that connection isn't enough, there's
a line of dialogue uttered by the Red Skull

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.980
that's meant to remind you of the classic
Indiana Jones flick.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:51.060
Early on in the movie, the Red Skull finds
the Tesseract in a town in Norway.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.960
"And the Fuhrer digs for trinkets in the desert."

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.970
Avid moviegoers will likely draw the connection
between this line and Hitler's quest to find

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:01.305
the Ark of the Covenant in Raiders.

00:04:01.305 --> 00:04:05.409
There's a scene in The Avengers that features
Captain America training at the gym.

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:09.640
Fury shows up with a briefing and an invitation
to join the Avengers Initiative.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:13.720
While Fury's papers contain important information,
it's the name of the agent who prepared it

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:18.510
that makes it even more fascinating than it
already is, at least for comic book fans.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.700
Upon closer inspection of the first page of
the document, the name Mephisto is in the

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.190
caption under the photo of the Tesseract.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.470
This codename appears to refer to the S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.850
operative who helped the agency acquire data
about the powerful artifact.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:34.950
However, some might have speculated that this
was a reference to the Marvel comics demon

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:39.440
of the same name, a malevolent, extradimensional
force who uses mystical means and trickery

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.520
to cause all sorts of mayhem in the Marvel
universe.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.730
Over time, Mephisto became an in-joke among
MCU fans because of all of the debunked conspiracy

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.190
theories about the demon's supposed involvement
in the events of WandaVision.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:55.947
But hey, at least we can say that the MCU
technically does have a Mephisto, kinda.

00:04:55.947 --> 00:05:01.810
During the battle between Iron Man and Aldrich
Killian, Tony lops off Killian's left hand.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:05.960
In Thor: The Dark World, Loki makes it appear
that he chopped off Thor's hand in order to

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.500
trick the Dark Elves.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.750
In Captain America: The Winter Soldier, Bucky
Barnes resurfaces as the villain, with his

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:13.680
entire left arm missing.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.120
In Guardians of the Galaxy, Groot loses both
arms because of Gamora, while Klaue gets his

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.780
arm swiftly chopped off by Ultron in Avengers:
Age of Ultron.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:26.790
Lastly, in Ant-Man, when Scott Lang disables
the Yellowjacket suit with Darren Cross still

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:30.590
inside it, the villain's right arm shrinks
first before the rest of him does.

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:35.340
In an interview with CinemaBlend, Kevin Feige
confirmed that the phase-wide Easter egg was

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.440
a nod to Star Wars Episode V: The Empire Strikes
Back.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:38.972
He said:

00:05:38.972 --> 00:05:42.556
"It didn't start out as intentional, but it
became intentional."

00:05:42.556 --> 00:05:47.910
In one scene of Thor: The Dark World, there's
a chalkboard full of notes from Dr. Erik Selvig

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.310
and Dr. Jane Foster related to the possible
destruction of Earth due to the cosmic event

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.090
called the Convergence.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:58.190
Eager fans noticed that the board contained
references to real-world science and Thor's

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:59.190
comic book lore.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:02.750
The book Thor: The Dark World – The Art
of the Movie, released in the same year, has

00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.530
a "laymanized" list of the items on the board.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:09.630
The chalkboard mentions phenomena related
to black holes, how matter changes from one

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:13.250
state to another, electromagnetic forces,
and even string theory.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:18.990
It also has some terms that comics readers
would doubtlessly recognize, such as 616 universe,

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.584
the Nexus of All Reality, and the Crossroads.

00:06:21.584 --> 00:06:22.593
"It's simple.

00:06:22.593 --> 00:06:23.778
Any questions?"

00:06:26.272 --> 00:06:31.520
Captain America: The Winter Soldier introduced
some significant changes to the MCU.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.600
For starters, it features Bucky as the Winter
Soldier and contains the first appearance

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.030
of the Falcon.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:40.970
Perhaps most importantly, it reveals the sinister
truth behind S.H.I.E.L.D., which had become

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.960
infested with Hydra agents.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.860
Nick Fury even has to fake his own death so
he can continue to work behind the scenes

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.220
and bring down the corrupted agency.

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:54.690
The Biblical passage engraved on his tombstone
ascribed to Ezekiel 25:17 will no doubt be

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.690
familiar to longtime fans of Samuel L. Jackson.

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:02.350
It's part of his dialogue in one of the most
iconic sequences from the famous 1994 crime

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:03.740
film Pulp Fiction.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:10.550
"The path of the righteous is beset on all
sides by the inequities of the selfish and

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:12.790
the tyranny of evil men."

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:16.750
In an interview, directors Joe and Anthony
Russo shared that they decided on the verse

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:20.287
following the suggestion of the film's effects
supervisor.

00:07:20.287 --> 00:07:25.220
For a brief period of time, there was speculation
that Stan Lee would not show up in Guardians

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:28.650
of the Galaxy due to the fact that he didn't
have a hand in their creation.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:32.970
That's why fans were delighted when the film's
final cut did in fact contain a cameo from

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:37.380
The Man himself, complete with his signature
line "Excelsior" flashing on the screen in

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:38.970
a made-up alien language.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:42.520
However, this was actually not the original
plan for Lee's cameo.

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:46.370
In an interview with director James Gunn,
he confirmed that initially, Lee was supposed

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:50.780
to be a specimen in the treasure room of the
Collector, and was supposed to throw a rude

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:52.060
gesture at Groot.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:56.360
Gunn also said that he decided to scrap the
cameo from the film on his own, and that it

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:58.332
wasn't a studio mandate as rumored.

00:07:58.332 --> 00:08:03.620
Avengers: Age of Ultron confirmed what many
audiences probably already knew about Tony

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:08.310
Stark: he had contingency plans in place if
the technology he frequently relied on ever

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:10.410
got disabled or compromised.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:12.120
Following the transfer of his previous A.I.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:17.370
assistant JARVIS into the body of Vision,
Stark was in need of a replacement.

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:20.410
Stark picks out FRIDAY, which becomes his
new guidance system.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:24.900
However, fans also caught a glimpse of some
of the program cards that Stark didn't select

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:28.970
— and two of them bore names that were quite
familiar to Marvel comics fans.

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:33.260
One of the cards was labeled "JOCASTA," which
longtime readers of the Avengers' comic books

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:37.410
would know is the name of the robot that was
built by Ultron to be his partner.

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:41.650
Meanwhile, another card bore the name "TADASHI,"
a reference to the inventor of the A.I.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:45.200
program behind Baymax in the Disney film Big
Hero 6.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:50.450
The organization known as the Ten Rings has
been an established threat in the background

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:52.180
of the MCU since Iron Man.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:58.100
However, what most fans may not know is that
in 2015, Ant-Man almost featured a blink-and-you'll-miss-it

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.880
reference to the terrorist faction in its
final cut.

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.290
In a commentary track for the film, Ant-Man
lead Paul Rudd and director Peyton Reed spill

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:08.360
the beans on a short scene that was omitted
from the movie.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:13.620
After Pym Technologies CEO Darren Cross was
able to unlock the shrinking secrets of Pym

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:18.680
Particles, he immediately invited a group
of prospective buyers to a demonstration at the company.

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:22.570
In the deleted scene, a tattoo of the Ten
Rings was partially visible on the neck of

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:26.390
one of the potential investors, implying that
he was actually a member who wanted to get

00:09:26.390 --> 00:09:28.585
his hands on the shrinking technology.

00:09:28.585 --> 00:09:33.401
During the big showdown between Team Cap and
Team Tony at the airport in Germany, there's

00:09:33.401 --> 00:09:37.450
a scene where Ant-Man and Captain America
use Pym Particles to turn a shrunken vehicle

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:39.770
into an expanding projectile.

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:43.880
For a few seconds, we get a glimpse of a unique
vehicle that should be instantly recognizable

00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:48.030
to fans of the sitcom Arrested Development
— the Bluth Company stair car, missing the

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:49.970
company's logo on the side.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:54.020
There's a good reason for this Easter egg:
the Russo brothers actually directed the pilot

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:58.780
of Arrested Development years before they
got into the business of directing MCU films.

00:09:58.780 --> 00:10:02.820
As fans of the show would attest, the Bluth
family car wasn't exactly known for being

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.390
a smooth ride, and was even used in a few
wacky situations.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:09.511
Let's just be thankful that it didn't cause
our heroes any trouble.

00:10:09.511 --> 00:10:14.530
Doctor Strange featured a simple but clever
gag that was even used in the film's trailer.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:18.580
When Stephen Strange arrives at the mystical
sanctuary in search of the Ancient One, her

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:23.150
student Mordo hands Strange a piece of paper
with the word "Shamballa" on it.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:27.710
He asks what it's for, instantly assuming
that it's some sort of magical incantation.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:28.750
Mordo's response?

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:31.090
"The WiFi password.

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:33.820
We're not savages."

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:37.430
Of course, because this is a Marvel movie,
you can bet that there's an interesting meaning

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:38.610
behind the word.

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:43.840
Published in 1986, the 23rd issue of the Marvel
Graphic Novel series features a story called

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.990
"Doctor Strange: Into Shamballa."

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.060
The book follows Dr. Strange as he travels
to the Ancient One's homeland and eventually

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.370
arrives at a magical moral quandary.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:56.290
We won't spoil the rest of the story for you,
so that you can read it yourself.

00:10:56.290 --> 00:11:01.480
When we see Stan the Man himself in "Guardians
Volume 2," he's wearing a spacesuit and sitting

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:02.660
on an asteroid.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:07.070
He's not alone, though, as three Watchers
are listening to him talk about other adventures.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:09.230
In other words, as one Twitter user put it:

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:15.760
"Stan Lee was canonically a Watcher informant,
and was playing the same character in every MCU movie."

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.340
Director James Gunn responded to this tweet
by admitting that "fan theories on Twitter"

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:22.910
were what prompted him to shoot this lighthearted
Stan Lee scene.

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:28.130
Kevin Feige himself acknowledged that the
late Marvel legend transcends the reality of the MCU.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:32.050
As cool as it is, this scene did create a
bit of a continuity error, though.

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:36.589
Lee mentions being a FedEx delivery man, which
was his cameo in Captain America: Civil War

00:11:36.589 --> 00:11:39.670
— a film that supposedly took place after
this one.

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:42.000
To his credit, Gunn acknowledged the error.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.440
There's a scene in Spider-Man: Homecoming
where Peter Parker makes use of voice-changing

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:50.620
Stark technology to sound more intimidating
during an interrogation attempt.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:54.150
Hilariously, it doesn't work on his target,
Aaron Davis.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.130
"Deactivate interrogation mode."

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:58.230
This sequence is special for two reasons.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:02.580
It teases the existence of another Spider-person,
and it's the culmination of a seven-year-old

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:06.090
campaign to put Donald Glover in a Spider-Man
movie.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:09.500
Davis is, of course, the uncle of Miles Morales,
a.k.a.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:13.980
the Spider-Man from the Ultimate universe
who now occupies the main Marvel Earth.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:19.029
While the MCU's Davis doesn't say Morales'
name, he does mention that he has a nephew,

00:12:19.029 --> 00:12:20.810
which is more than enough confirmation.

00:12:20.810 --> 00:12:26.279
Back in 2010, when the Spider-Man reboot was
announced, #donald4spiderman went viral on

00:12:26.279 --> 00:12:29.910
Twitter as Glover's fans pushed for him to
be considered for the lead role.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:33.580
Of course, Andrew Garfield was eventually
cast as the webslinger.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:37.820
Years later, Spider-Man: Homecoming director
Jon Watts swore to find a way to include Glover

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:41.644
in his Spider-film — and the rest, as they
say, is history.

00:12:41.644 --> 00:12:46.660
In Thor: Ragnarok, Thor finds himself trapped
on the waste planet of Sakaar.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:50.320
He becomes an unwilling participant in the
Contest of Champions.

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:54.160
The Grandmaster honors previous victors by
adding their sculptures on the tower of his

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:57.380
palace, which already has a few familiar faces
on it.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:01.430
Eagle-eyed fans will have noticed the faces
of the mystical guardian the Man-Thing, the

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:06.710
war god Ares, and the Hulk villains Bi-Beast
and Dark-Crawler on the tower.

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:11.070
The most significant face there belongs to
none other than Beta Ray Bill, a horse-faced,

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:16.279
genetically modified member of the Korbinite
race and ally of Thor in the comics who wields

00:13:16.279 --> 00:13:18.279
the powerful weapon Stormbreaker.

00:13:18.279 --> 00:13:23.540
Kevin Feige revealed that Beta Ray Bill originally
had a slightly bigger role in Thor: Ragnarok,

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.020
but he was cut from the film.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:26.370
As he told Crave Online,

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:30.833
"The feeling is, if you can't do [a character
like Beta Ray Bill] justice, do it later."

00:13:30.833 --> 00:13:36.380
When T'Challa successfully subdues Ulysses
Klaue after a long chase, the villain begs

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.630
for mercy from the Wakandan king.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:39.630
The superhero responds,

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.630
"Every breath you take is mercy from me."

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:46.839
It's an awesome line that, unbeknownst to
many moviegoers, is actually taken from New

00:13:46.839 --> 00:13:51.270
Avengers Volume 3 Number 22, in which T'Challa
says it to Namor.

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:56.380
T'Challa already had a major ax to grind with
the arrogant Atlantean king before that issue,

00:13:56.380 --> 00:14:00.670
as Namor once caused tremendous suffering
to many Wakandans as a result of what could

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:02.990
charitably be called a "petty grudge."

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:07.040
By the time the two kings found themselves
on the same team, their animosity had already

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:11.540
reached a boiling point, leading to a brutal
confrontation — and Namor's abrupt departure

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:12.540
from the team.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:17.471
While audiences were happy to see Spider-Man
become formally integrated into the MCU with

00:14:17.471 --> 00:14:21.780
the release of Spider-Man: Homecoming, the
apparent absence of his famous spider-sense

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:24.560
throughout the film became a serious point
of contention.

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:29.320
In a 2017 interview with Collider, Spider-Man:
Homecoming director Jon Watts explained the

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:32.870
decision to make the spider-sense less of
a presence in his film.

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:33.870
He said,

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:38.230
"The idea was, again, just you want to make
this movie be less about things you've already

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:42.770
seen before, and you've definitely already
seen a spider-sense sequence done extremely

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.310
well in Marc [Webb]'s and [Sam] Raimi's movies."

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:49.600
Despite Kevin Feige's confirmation that the
MCU version of Peter definitely possesses

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:53.910
a spider-sense and that they would "explore
it further down the line," fans still eagerly

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:58.770
awaited the day they'd witness this version
of the webslinger's spidey-sense in action.

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:02.560
That day eventually came in Avengers: Infinity
War — although Aunt May would eventually

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:04.460
refer to it by a different name.

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:07.171
"Please do not start calling it my 'Peter
tingle.'"

00:15:07.171 --> 00:15:11.680
While most of Ant-Man and the Wasp explores
Scott's life under house arrest following

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:16.500
his participation in the Civil War battle,
it also introduces another size-changing superhero

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:17.640
from the comics.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:20.160
Dr. Bill Foster, aka Goliath.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:24.350
The movie doesn't really show Foster engaging
in heroics the same way Ant-Man and the Wasp

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:29.730
do, but it does mention his time working with
Dr. Hank Pym as a S.H.I.E.L.D. scientist.

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:33.200
In the scene where Dr. Foster is shown lecturing
to a classroom full of students, the chalkboard

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:35.740
behind him is full of mathematical equations.

00:15:35.740 --> 00:15:39.649
It also has the word "matrix" written on it,
which, while a perfectly appropriate word

00:15:39.649 --> 00:15:43.880
for the situation, can also be interpreted
as a reference to Fishburne's iconic role

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:46.043
as Morpheus in The Matrix.

00:15:46.043 --> 00:15:50.950
Marvel legend Stan Lee was able to film a
few extra cameo appearances for a handful

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:54.060
of Marvel movies before his death in 2018.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.470
One such Easter egg was for Captain Marvel.

00:15:56.470 --> 00:16:01.220
During the sequence where Carol Danvers searches
for a Skrull agent in Los Angeles, she briefly

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:03.840
encounters Lee sitting by one of the windows.

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:08.210
The comic book writer is shown reading the
script for the 1995 film Mallrats, which he

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:09.450
also appeared in.

00:16:09.450 --> 00:16:13.190
Speaking to The Hollywood Reporter, Mallrats
director Kevin Smith expressed his delight

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:16.720
at the nature of Lee's cameo in Captain Marvel,
and how it referenced his film.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:20.940
"It was really kind of poetic and to hear
him saying his line, which, to be fair, was

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:24.790
some s–t that he would say in real life
all the time: 'Trust me, true believer.'

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:26.029
It was everything."

00:16:26.029 --> 00:16:30.290
Smith must have been thrilled by the fact
that this was basically confirmation that

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:32.876
a version of him exists in the MCU.

00:16:32.876 --> 00:16:37.330
One of the best things about Avengers: Endgame
is the way the main characters are able to

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:39.430
revisit some of their most iconic moments.

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:43.089
In the case of Steve Rogers, his visit to
the past allowed him to play things a bit

00:16:43.089 --> 00:16:47.589
smarter in a situation very similar to that
elevator fight scene from The Winter Soldier.

00:16:47.589 --> 00:16:52.160
This time, instead of beating up an elevator
full of Hydra agents, Rogers is able to bypass

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:54.050
a violent confrontation.

00:16:54.050 --> 00:16:56.410
"Hail Hydra."

00:16:56.410 --> 00:17:03.510
This works, and Rogers is seen seconds later
exiting the elevator with the scepter.

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:07.680
While this scene doubtlessly calls back to
Cap's elevator fight, it's also a sly reference

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:10.770
to a controversial 2016 comic.

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:15.589
Captain America: Steve Rogers Number 1, in
which Rogers is revealed as a secret Hydra

00:17:15.589 --> 00:17:18.870
agent, a move that unsurprisingly riled fans
of the character.

00:17:18.870 --> 00:17:22.880
That backlash was so fierce that Marvel had
to ask for readers to be patient while the

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:24.348
story unfolded.

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:29.260
When Peter Parker was officially introduced
to the MCU in Civil War, the character also

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:33.960
revealed a bit about his origin during a candid
conversation with Tony Stark.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:37.790
Their talk made it clear that it was some
sort of grave personal tragedy that served

00:17:37.790 --> 00:17:41.770
as Peter's wake-up call, making him realize
that he needs to "fight for the little guy."

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:45.520
This seemed to hint at his existence, but
there was no formal mention of Peter's uncle

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.410
Ben Parker in Spider-Man: Homecoming.

00:17:47.410 --> 00:17:52.690
Fans didn't like the omission of arguably
the most important figure in Spider-Man's world.

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:54.900
Perhaps that's why the inclusion of the initials
"B.F.P."

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:59.800
on Peter's travel briefcase in Spider-Man:
Far From Home was so well-received: it's the

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:05.507
first real evidence that Uncle Ben existed
in the MCU, as well as a memorable Easter egg.

00:18:05.507 --> 00:18:10.100
Black Widow gave MCU fans the opportunity
to know more about Natasha Romanoff's past

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:15.030
and adoptive family, as well as what she got
up to after the events of Civil War.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:19.280
Interestingly, it also answered a question
that few people probably bothered to ask:

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:21.610
How did she get that cool-looking vest?

00:18:21.610 --> 00:18:27.730
As it turns out, the vest belonged to Natasha's
sister and fellow Black Widow operative, Yelena Belova.

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:31.350
The younger Widow proudly shares to her big
sister, whom she has not seen for more than

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:35.870
two decades, that the vest is the first thing
she ever bought for herself.

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:39.050
After the final showdown, the two sisters
say their loving goodbyes, but not before

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:42.290
Belova leaves her vest with Romanoff as a
parting gift.

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:46.460
The touching gesture confirms that the bond
between them remains strong as ever.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.650
Arguably, this also made Belova's reaction
to the news of Romanoff's death so much more

00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:52.507
emotional and believable.

00:18:52.782 --> 00:18:56.870
In the scene where Shang-Chi and Katy enter
the Golden Daggers Club, they are given a

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:58.980
quick tour of the place by the host.

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:03.330
While most MCU fans will have no doubt spotted
the fight between the Abomination and Wong,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:08.180
there is another noteworthy fight featuring
two combatants deeply tied to MCU history.

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:11.770
In one of the cells where the fights take
place, a Black Widow who was rescued during

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:15.870
the events of the Black Widow movie is seen
squaring off against a soldier whose body

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:18.730
has been enhanced by the virus from Iron Man
3.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:22.790
While the Widow appears to get the upper hand
by using her knives, she is later seen tending

00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:27.060
to the soldier's wounds, reinforcing that
not all of the combatants in the club see

00:19:27.060 --> 00:19:29.270
each other as enemies outside of the ring.

00:19:29.270 --> 00:19:33.920
The second post-credits scene of Eternals
sets up the introduction of not one, but two

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:35.650
classic Marvel heroes.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:40.220
After the disappearance of Sersi, her beau
Dane Whitman is seen opening a box containing

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:44.679
what appears to be a family heirloom: a sword
that has clearly been kept wrapped and hidden

00:19:44.679 --> 00:19:46.010
for a long time.

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:50.230
This is, of course, the Ebony Blade, the weapon
that Whitman wields as the medieval-themed

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:52.450
superhero the Black Knight.

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:56.020
As the nervous human attempts to touch the
blade, he hears a voice saying,

00:19:56.020 --> 00:20:00.840
"Sure you're ready for that, Mr. Whitman?"

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:05.929
The scene abruptly ends without revealing
who the mysterious voice belongs to.

00:20:05.929 --> 00:20:10.590
In an interview with Empire Online, director
Chloé Zhao confirmed that the line was spoken

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.870
by Mahershala Ali, the MCU's version of Blade.

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:14.870
She said,

00:20:14.870 --> 00:20:19.040
"I gotta say, in just the little time I spent
with Mahershala, I can't wait for the Blade

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.040
movie to come out."

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:25.060
Given the sheer amount of nostalgic references
incorporated into Spider-Man: No Way Home,

00:20:25.060 --> 00:20:28.020
it's hard to choose the "best" among all of
its Easter eggs.

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:32.060
For instance, the entire conversation between
the three Peters during the climactic battle

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:34.490
is, in itself, one Easter egg after another.

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:38.830
The bit where Andrew Garfield offers to "crack"
Maguire's back to relieve its stiffness is

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:43.290
a clever nod to Maguire's infamous back problems
during the development of Spider-Man 2.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:47.560
The filmmakers even managed to sneak in a
scene where the three Peters recreate that

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.830
famous pointing meme from the 1960s Spider-Man
cartoon.

00:20:50.830 --> 00:20:54.990
However, if we had to pick just one Easter
egg as the best of the lot, it would have

00:20:54.990 --> 00:20:58.980
to be how Garfield's Spider-Man saves MJ from
falling to her death.

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:03.340
As fans who saw The Amazing Spider-Man 2 will
recall, he was unable to save the love of

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:07.350
his life, Gwen Stacy, who died in a strikingly
similar situation.

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:11.110
It nearly drove him to hang up the webs for
good — and by saving the girlfriend of an

00:21:11.110 --> 00:21:15.110
alternate-reality version of himself, he gained
some much-needed closure.

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:19.977
Garfield says a lot with his face alone in
this scene, one of the standout moments in the movie.

00:21:19.977 --> 00:21:24.950
When Doctor Strange and America Chavez go
multiverse-hopping, they become stranded on

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:30.590
Earth-838, where an alternate version of Strange
seemingly sacrificed his life to defeat Thanos.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:34.700
However, the traveling duo are detained in
the Baxter Foundation, where the mainstream

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:38.440
MCU Strange learns the horrific truth about
his counterpart's death.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:42.710
Here, Strange also meets the Illuminati, a
covert group of heroes who secretly determine

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:45.750
the best course of action against major threats.

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:50.809
The Illuminati of Earth-838 is made up of
Mordo, Captain Marvel, the Inhuman king Black

00:21:50.809 --> 00:21:56.500
Bolt, Reed Richards of the Fantastic Four,
Captain Carter, and their leader, Professor Charles Xavier.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:22:00.700
This incarnation of Professor X sits in his
yellow hoverchair from the '90s comics.

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:05.858
Additionally, when he is introduced, a brief
segment of the iconic X-Men: The Animated Series theme plays.

00:22:05.858 --> 00:22:12.040
In one scene of Thor: Love and Thunder, Zeus
uses his godly powers to "flick" away a restrained Thor's garments.

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:16.250
He overdoes it, though, which leaves Thor
thoroughly exposed to the stunned crowd.

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:20.210
We get a good view of Thor from behind, which
shows that he has a big tattoo on his back

00:22:20.210 --> 00:22:21.820
to honor his brother Loki.

00:22:21.820 --> 00:22:25.450
Right next to it is another tattoo shaped
like a roll of parchment, bearing some of

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:28.280
the names of Thor's loved ones who have passed
away.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:31.880
His mom and dad are there, as are Heimdall,
Tony, and Natasha.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:36.865
Interestingly, according to director Taika
Waititi, this Easter egg was cut from Thor: Ragnarok.

00:22:36.865 --> 00:22:41.020
However, they deemed it too good to waste,
so they made it bigger for Thor: Love and Thunder.

